euler
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.000
The source of derived-order's precedence rule, from the author's paste of Christopher's EULER notes (Thomas W. Christopher, 1996; EULER: Wirth and Weber, "EULER: a generalization of ALGOL, and its formal definition", Communications of the ACM, 1966): the simplified EULER grammar, transcribed as given into PLAN9/wsn/euler.wsn (the scanner's id, number, symbol as literals), read by zprec in Plan 9: no erasing rules, no useless rules, but 35 Wirth-Weber conflicts (VAR left-recursive under "@", "isb" ... "isu" and EXPR's closers, among them) and PROGRAM and EXPR sharing the right side BLOCK: not a simple precedence grammar. As Christopher writes: the original's simple precedence parser required pairs of names (sum and sum-, term and term-), which his more powerful parser lets him remove. So zprec reproduces the source's own account.

stack-element
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.000
zcast's open exchange, from the author's paste (again, 2026-10-28) of "Figure 1: A CSP specification of a stack element". The paste lost each line's ?, ! and ; to loose rows; restored by the protocol: E [ in?! -> in?x ; F [] out?! -> get?x ; out!x ; E ] and F [ out?! -> out!x ; E [] in?! -> put!x ; in?x ; F ], using three ?, three ! and six ;, exactly what the loose rows hold. Empty, asked in, it takes x and is full; asked out, it gets x from the element below and passes it out, still empty. Full, asked out, it gives x and is empty; asked in, it puts x down to the element below and takes the new one, still full. Its finite traces from empty are PLAN9/wsn/element.wsn, read by zlazy in Plan 9 (element.rc): 5 traces of the protocol accepted, 4 that break it refused (out!x when empty without get, put when empty, two in?x without a put, get when full); zprec: an erasing rule, the [ ] a trace that may stop needs, so not a simple precedence grammar. The figure's paper is not named in the paste.

